{
  "policy_id": "ref-17-abolish-ir35-rules",
  "party": "Reform UK",
  "policy_title": "Abolish IR35 rules",
  "outcome": "O12",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O12",
    "direction": "worsens",
    "magnitude": "moderate",
    "time_horizon": "this-parliament",
    "confidence": "moderate",
    "plain": "Abolishing IR35 would remove rules that currently raise several billion pounds a year in tax, widening the gap between what employees and self-employed workers pay. Without an alternative mechanism to replace that revenue, the bill would fall on either public services or future borrowing.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether abolition would be accompanied by a broader reform merging income tax and NICs that could recover the lost revenue through other means.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy proposes to abolish IR35 rules entirely to support sole traders.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "Reform UK will abolish IR35 rules to support sole traders"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "IR35 rules aim to ensure contractors pay broadly the same Income Tax and NICs as employees, countering tax avoidance.",
        "evidence_id": "E1",
        "quote": "The legislation aims to ensure these workers pay broadly the same Income Tax and National Insurance Contributions (NICs) as employees, thereby countering tax avoidance"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "HMRC estimated the 2017 and 2021 reforms raised around \u00a34.2 billion in additional tax and NICs in three years to March 2023.",
        "evidence_id": "E8",
        "quote": "HMRC estimated that the 2017 and 2021 reforms raised around \u00a34.2 billion in additional tax and NICs in the three years to March 2023"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "The Resolution Foundation estimates a typical self-employed worker pays approximately 55% less in total labour tax than an employee earning the same gross pay.",
        "evidence_id": "E7",
        "quote": "a typical self-employed worker pays approximately 55% less in total labour tax than an employee earning the same gross pay (2025-26 estimate), representing an 11-percentage-point tax gap in gross income"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "The cancelled 2022 repeal of the IR35 reforms was estimated to cost around \u00a32 billion a year.",
        "evidence_id": "E9",
        "quote": "The Resolution Foundation noted the cancelled 2022 repeal would have cost around \u00a32 billion a year"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "The estimated cost of the short-lived 2022 repeal over five years was \u00a36.19 billion.",
        "evidence_id": "E6",
        "quote": "The estimated cost of that repeal was \u00a36.19 billion over five years"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "The IFS and OTS suggest a comprehensive reform of the tax system is needed rather than simply removing IR35 without an alternative.",
        "evidence_id": "E27",
        "quote": "the IFS and OTS, suggest that the core issue lies in the differential tax treatment of employment and self-employment, and a more comprehensive reform of the tax system is needed rather than simply removing IR35 without an alternative"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "IR35 currently functions as a revenue-generating anti-avoidance mechanism. The measurable evidence is clear: the 2017 and 2021 reforms raised approximately \u00a34.2 billion in additional tax over three years, and independent estimates put the annual cost of repealing those reforms at around \u00a32 billion per year. The structural driver is the large existing tax gap between employees and the self-employed \u2014 roughly 55% less in total labour tax \u2014 which IR35 partially closes for 'disguised employees'. Abolition without a replacement mechanism would reopen this gap at scale. The policy text contains no funded offset, no replacement revenue instrument, and no commitment to the kind of broader income-tax/NICs merger that the IFS and OTS suggest could make IR35 redundant in a fiscally neutral way. The 2022 repeal attempt \u2014 reversed within weeks partly due to fiscal concerns \u2014 provides a real-world precedent: even a partial rollback was scored as a multi-billion-pound cost. On O12's criteria \u2014 is spending (or in this case, a tax expenditure) funded, and does it worsen the debt path? \u2014 the answer leans clearly toward 'worsens'. The only plausible countervailing argument is that abolition boosts contractor activity and tax receipts indirectly; but no cited evidence quantifies this as sufficient to offset the direct revenue loss, and the IFS explicitly warns against piecemeal removal without systemic reform. Magnitude is moderate: the annual cost is material but not transformative relative to total fiscal aggregates. Confidence is moderate because the exact behavioural response to abolition is uncertain, but the direction of the fiscal effect is not.",
